Flagged in a Linus-style code review pass, not discovered by dogfooding this time.

git-notes-grep.sh walked the full `git log` and forked `git notes show` once per commit, discarding almost all of them -- O(all commits) subprocess spawns to find O(notes) results. `git notes list` already gives exactly the commit set that has notes in one call, with no full-history walk. Switched to that; the only remaining per-hit cost is one `git log -1` (for the sort timestamp) and one `git notes show`/`git log -1 --format=%s` per actual match, not per commit in history. Deliberately avoided `declare -A` for the commit-sha lookup even though it would have been simpler, for the same reason mapfile was avoided on 8af5907: macOS ships bash 3.2, which has neither.

Also fixed: keywords were passed straight into `grep -E` unescaped, so a search for "v1.0" silently matched "v1X0" too (`.` is "any char" in ERE) and a search for "foo(bar" would error out on the unbalanced paren instead of finding anything. Added a portable char-by-char escape_regex() (no sed, no external tool, same bash-3.2 constraint) so keywords are matched literally, as the usage text always implied they would be.

Shortened the PostToolUse hook's additionalContext from a full restatement of SKILL.md's note criteria (~600 characters) to a one-line pointer ("Check it against SKILL.md's git-why note criteria; skip if mechanical.", ~100 characters). The original reasoning for spelling out the criteria was to make the reminder self-contained even if SKILL.md weren't loaded, but that's re-paying the same ~500 characters into context on every single commit for the life of a session, for information that doesn't change. Since the hook only exists to guarantee the check happens, not to teach the check, a pointer back to SKILL.md is sufficient and cheaper.
